# component-bin.pkg -- the RUNTIME half of a component: the files that run.
#
# ONE DECLARATION, EVERY COMPONENT.  `@C@' stands for the component name, so
# this file describes c900-net-bin-v3.5.0.tar.gz and c900-mgr-bin-v3.5.0.tar.gz
# and every other one.  The alternative was eleven near-identical .pkg files,
# which is the shape the toolchain's two host archives were in when the Windows
# zip lost host/ and no gate noticed.  A kind gains a component the day a
# dist/lists/*.list gains a `package' line, and nothing here has to be written
# again.
#
# WHAT IT IS.  A component is a dist/lists/*.list that is a package: base,
# runtime, net, mail-light, games, net-games, mgr, mgr-clients, editors,
# archive.  That list, resolved against its producer's build output, IS this
# package -- so what a component-bin package installs and what an image carries
# are the same files by construction, not by two lists that have to be kept
# level.  The kernel repository's console-* components are cut to this same
# declaration by its own os/hostbuild/pack-component.sh: one format, wherever
# the files were built.
#
# manifest.tab is the whole of the installation instruction: `<type> <path>
# <mode> <uid> <gid>', the six columns of `component.py component', minus the
# source column, which named a path on the machine that packed it and means
# nothing afterwards.  A tar archive cannot carry a COHERENT uid/gid or a setuid bit
# through an unpack on somebody's Linux box, so the modes are DATA here and the
# installer applies them; an installer that reads the tar bits alone would give
# /usr/bin/screen 0755 instead of 6511 and it would silently not work.
kind      component-bin
what      one component's runtime files: what it installs and the modes to install them with
producer  dist/pack-component.sh
archive   c900-@C@-bin-v@V@.tar.gz
toplevel  c900-@C@-bin-v@V@
version   VERSION
stamp     .provenance  kind commit version package component pkgkind entries contentid
text      .provenance  ^package=@C@-bin$
text      .provenance  ^pkgkind=bin$
text      .provenance  ^component=@C@$
file      LICENSE
# ---- THE TERMS THE CONTENTS ARE UNDER ----
# LICENSE above is what this repository says about its OWN files.  What the
# component's CONTENTS are under travels with the release, not inside each
# archive: the texts are collected once into `licences' beside the packages,
# with an index.tab saying which regimes govern which component.  A source
# package carries each program's own COPYING beside the code as well, because
# it ships the source root upstream put it in.
file      manifest.tab
dir       files
glob      files/*  1
# The manifest is not decoration: every payload path is a row of it, and
# check-contents.sh (below) is what asserts the two agree.  A package whose
# files/ and manifest.tab disagree installs a set nobody declared.
text      manifest.tab  ^f /
# ---- WHICH BYTES ----
# .contents is md5sum's own format over every other file here and
# .provenance:contentid is its sha1, exactly as the toolchain packages do it, so
# a flipped byte is caught inside the archive and named.  There is no source id:
# a component-bin package is a SELECTION of the bytes a producer built, and the
# stamp names THE SET IT SELECTED FROM, by the id that set is known by.  Two
# producers cut this kind: for this repository's components it is `ulid', the
# content id publish-userland.sh records over the published tree, so a package
# can be matched to the sweep that produced it; for the kernel repository's
# console drivers it is `kernel_linkid', the id of the kernel `ld -k' bound them
# to, which is the only kernel they load into.
text      .provenance  ^(ulid|kernel_linkid)=
file      .contents
file      check-contents.sh
idfile    .contents  .provenance  contentid
gate      check-contents.sh
